#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "b3b024c9-282e-55c3-9300-0db6b88cd3c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6609" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y = 20 e ^ {3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1, -2,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $20e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3 \\alpha e ^{3x},\n\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 9  \\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha (9+3-2){e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =20{e}^{3x} \\\\ 10\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is \n\n$$\ny_p = 2e^{3x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "0d5c1b8c-6f6e-53d6-b2de-034a3f193fef" :question/math-academy-id "e-6610" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/46/4652a17e8836f9ea84df971537f3c0df1f5ccd2b2dfd7d8d9615ef69a775b972.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the roots of the characteristic equation are ![](images/bb/bb796871deb9ebb6ff0ac3c98715a74275b8957374753a1bfed998898de136f8.png) we get\n![](images/fb/fbd5028588b1b3639ae465a5904a49e354da5f77ae0224b398666449dcfd809b.png)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](images/4d/4d6d063a30dc8f4bab52eab0764e8dee217ddd74055d79cae2bcca34e46ca45f.png) and ![](images/df/df86a6554579076b0783330da9971ee6ac847ce0d7615c81da959cc197372021.png) is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n![](images/18/187230e4c3f0192e65cb84e69fa12ece83467ae67ca3f73f09ae636d8eb7daad.png)\n\nwhere ![](images/13/13751f284d6fa34631cd1eb362665db0f1e29de98a1539e514009e7c5034e855.png) is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](images/63/6316c498377eadd30922f7f5ef6cfa1015cb3c059349bccf2aec7ca52ea5356e.png) we get\n\n![](images/95/955d1d9ea20426db629258e32703eda1f724e0787bedc68c653eaa684a997f02.png)\n\nTo find the value of ![](images/07/07f02913cce959d6fde7770ff94b1fbab15d24d8c7f7ee5c8cba48873d955f86.png)  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n![](images/07/0770a9b23e24c99631cdbc12378388ff089585206f0aea21ebfb3f65217cd4a2.png)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n![](images/67/67180bb752c7046f8b83aadacd7753bed9d827516c4f1930b341873b5399951e.png)\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n![](images/db/dbf2415e9f21a606aa92b6e26582cc1790470343346e967a0ff621c678560fd6.png)\n\nand the general solution is\n![](images/a1/a197e920c5e9c7d6b16fffcd7955fe07a212b92fb50636feae2c771f91a68f8a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "d655af30-3da8-50cb-8c06-c3860bf88b5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6611" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. Since the characteristic equation has a single repeated root $\\lambda = 3,$ the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ and $xe^{3x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2e ^ {3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y_p$, $y'_p$ and $y_p''$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{x}^{2}+12x+2)\\alpha {e}^{3x}-6(3{x}^{2}+2x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+9\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ (9{x}^{2}+12x+2-18{x}^{2}-12x+9{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =1 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac 1 2x^2 e ^{3x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf37829-4b55-5a39-bf20-d067b34db8fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-6787" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\nAe^{3 x} + Bxe^{3 x} + \\dfrac 1 2 x ^2 e^{3 x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 6\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 9y = e ^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) = 2 , \\ \\ y'(0) = 1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{0}+B(0){e}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 2 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{3x}+3Bx{e}^{3x}+x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(0)=1$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3A{e}^{0}+B{e}^{0}+3B(0){e}^{0}+0{e}^{0}+\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}{e}^{0} \\\\ 1 & =3A+B \\\\ 1 & =3(2)+B \\\\ -5 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-5,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2{e}^{3x}-5x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "583d410d-04f1-5059-ae2d-f61a6fe7e9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349256} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9ed6bd-defa-5191-afec-ca21413f9a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349449} {:answer/id #uuid "8a78a1c3-fb64-53f8-b86f-3f77711dc33a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349703} {:answer/id #uuid "c2671d11-442e-51d7-a66a-492a211dc05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}-{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350173} {:answer/id #uuid "defb955e-8b3b-5d59-a201-8980e6bb9ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "583d410d-04f1-5059-ae2d-f61a6fe7e9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349256} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8bc506-cf0d-5153-9c60-9111cee0e1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "703bb75c-b102-5bd2-876e-82612d9b3036" :question/math-academy-id "q-8101" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=21{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $21{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+7(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})+12(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9+21+12)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ 42\\alpha  & =21 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087871 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869c0701-5f5c-56de-bd55-f274ba6b34b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4x} + Bxe^{4x} + 2xe^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087000} {:answer/id #uuid "879e158a-113b-59c2-889e-9dc4ea494ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4x} + Bxe^{4x} + 3x^{2}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087001} {:answer/id #uuid "a5605efa-6ac7-5e11-876b-9032384648a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4x} + Be^{-4x} + xe^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087002} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7cd21b-ab87-51fd-ae55-ae8dc18bf715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4x} + Bxe^{4x} - x^{2}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087003} {:answer/id #uuid "773ebe21-0ad1-5411-997f-065e5f2f1a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4x} + Be^{-4x} - x^{2}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087004}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c81e5a-7c41-5cf7-aaef-60bd1c837976" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086999}] :question/id #uuid "22d4aef5-fb21-57f9-83fe-23733435808c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8105" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 8\\frac{dy}{dx} + 16y = 6e^{4x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e164011-eb35-591c-9a55-ea7723edbb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349155} {:answer/id #uuid "5826b528-62db-509a-8707-670b6578d7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349255} {:answer/id #uuid "715013e1-a0ea-59e7-8f0e-1dea7671cf79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349492} {:answer/id #uuid "b55e0689-26fc-5eab-9e1b-11dc8407cd13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350051} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fde841-1b91-56d8-87c8-58e7596aaa43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55e0689-26fc-5eab-9e1b-11dc8407cd13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350051} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bdd8ce2-b031-531f-854e-7b74f8227bab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c1cbd4b-2b58-54b2-8818-d30f2297ce38" :question/math-academy-id "q-8109" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=20{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2,$ which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-8+8){e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2P+2Q+10 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4$ and $Q=0.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0)+5{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087869 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b9aca3-96f6-56d8-bf46-91df9e2cee46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404553} {:answer/id #uuid "c467d5d8-892e-5503-84ae-3f3235dba85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-3x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404554} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1ec65d-9105-5c41-abc1-a34560fa51bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404555} {:answer/id #uuid "045fd68a-57ca-585a-9e45-01698b432866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404556} {:answer/id #uuid "e496e4d3-4727-5c25-b373-87c737214851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc1ec65d-9105-5c41-abc1-a34560fa51bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404555} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc90dc2-e777-5193-ab45-30485587cbf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "667003ae-52b1-572c-b234-cf239bc6b6d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-93675" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $12{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+4(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-8+8)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =12{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e93b6fb8-da4a-5b35-bd94-7c7b17d89869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404560} {:answer/id #uuid "694feab2-1865-543c-a32e-99cc6da36cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404561} {:answer/id #uuid "361fdeae-a507-5b99-bc39-5eed03c84e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-5x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404562} {:answer/id #uuid "34e11367-aa02-59ea-b0f7-6de755b9c9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-5{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404563} {:answer/id #uuid "cf037a78-f8ae-5aad-9516-009441200fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf037a78-f8ae-5aad-9516-009441200fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404564} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a0bc794-25e1-51cf-8047-1c1bc0cb1fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0b0d7eeb-30c2-58fb-b039-5edb4ff9136a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93678" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=10{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "Since the complementary function is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{4x},$ the roots of the auxiliary equation are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =4.$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (x-2){e}^{-x}-3(\\alpha (1-x){e}^{-x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{-x}) & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ (x-2-3+3x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =10{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087870 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0280f284-2b43-53d0-a98d-87e7d4fc25cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354678} {:answer/id #uuid "5c467016-23d4-54ad-ba9c-738b2aa552ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355478} {:answer/id #uuid "61f2318e-37cc-5d44-b734-4ecc041bf42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355534} {:answer/id #uuid "7a73ed70-0553-580c-9d5a-d146f6723f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355736} {:answer/id #uuid "ea86714b-1782-5dfd-a8ce-5b62ad843b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c467016-23d4-54ad-ba9c-738b2aa552ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355478} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbb148c-2f17-5a07-a49b-867dda2fb1a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7a39dd3-bf0f-5600-825b-b3087670bb2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93680" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=6{e}^{3x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (9x+6){e}^{3x}-5(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})+6(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}) & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9x+6-5-15x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =6{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+6x{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}]}
